Thermal strain, constraints and residual stress (@mech-thermal-strain: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W", #source "../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY", #mechanical-engineering "../structures/Axial-members-compatibility-and-load-sharing.lingua#r_1VCFFVTFXWNAS0G6CJC2B8DVKN") { r_0755DVWWAGZWYN0R75V7EDBR61
**Read:** [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), section 3.2 and the energy methods of chapter 5; [Modules in Mechanics of Materials — David Roylance](../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY), Constitutive Relations. Connect the temperature field to the thermal subject by reference when a uniform temperature is inadequate.

A uniform isotropic thermal expansion contributes αΔT to each normal strain in the simple constant-coefficient model. Mechanical stress depends on the strain left after subtracting this free expansion. A freely expanding bar can have nonzero strain and zero stress; a restrained bar can have zero total axial strain and nonzero stress.

For a uniform bar held at fixed axial length with lateral expansion unconstrained, σ = −EαΔT. E = 200 GPa, α = 12×10⁻⁶ K⁻¹ and ΔT = 50 K give −120 MPa. This assumes elastic behavior, a temperature-independent modulus/coefficient over the interval, perfect axial restraint and no buckling. With finite support stiffness, solve compatibility and force balance instead.

Residual stress persists after the external loading or heating that created it has changed. Plastic deformation, thermal gradients, phase changes and assembly interference can generate self-equilibrated stresses. A zero net external force does not prove zero internal stress.

**Work:** Compare free expansion, perfect restraint and a finite-stiffness support. For two bonded materials, derive force balance and compatible extension with different free strains. Check how support motion, uneven temperature or yielding changes the conclusion before applying a tabulated expansion coefficient to an assembly.

